.TH CLRU_GET 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
clru_get \- get a value by key (a type-inferred convenience wrapper)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clrucache.h>

ccol_retval_t clru_get(name, key, val_ptr);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
A type-inferred GNU C statement expression that converts key to the
cache's declared KeyT, the same way a plain C assignment does, looks
up the stored value and delivers it through val_ptr. What happens on
success depends on the value type that you declared when the cache
was constructed:

A val type other than char*: the stored value is converted to the
type of *val_ptr, as a plain C assignment would convert it. For
example, a cache that stores int values and reads into a double *
out-parameter converts the int to a double instead of copying the raw
bytes of the int. Nothing is allocated on the heap. val_ptr is a
pointer to the type of *val_ptr, NOT a cmap_pair *. If the compiler
cannot implicitly convert the cache's declared ValT to the type of
*val_ptr, that is a compile error at this point, never a value that
the macro silently reinterprets.

A char* val type: the macro hands the ownership of the heap string
straight to the caller through *(char **)val_ptr. The caller must free
that pointer with the cache's custom free function if a custom
allocator was configured at construction, and with free() if not.

Call clrucache_get_full() directly only when you need the raw
cmap_pair output, which clru_get() hides completely.

A cache with a character-pointer key type takes a const char * key
without a cast, because the macro keeps the pointer type of such a
key.

A key of a struct type is the same key as another one only when every
byte of the two is equal, padding included; see KEYS WITH PADDING in
clrucache_create_full(3). With GCC 11 or later the macro sets the
padding of its own copy of the key to zero, so a key with equal
members finds the entry that clru_set() stored.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name     Cache variable. The companion type variables, which come from
         clru_construct, clru_declare or clru_redeclare, must be in
         scope. When a cache crosses a function boundary, call
         clru_redeclare() with a simple local alias first, because
         converting the key type and the value type needs the
         companion variables name##__clru_key_type_var and
         name##__clru_val_type_var.
key      Key expression: any expression that the compiler can convert
         implicitly to KeyT.
val_ptr  A pointer to the value type, NOT a cmap_pair *.
         For a val type other than char*, it gets the stored value
         converted to the type of *val_ptr, for example a double *
         for a double out-parameter, and nothing is allocated on the
         heap. *val_ptr is left untouched when the call does not
         return ccol_success.
         For a char* val type, it points to a char* variable. On
         success the macro sets that variable to the heap string,
         and the caller must free the string with the matching
         allocator.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success           The macro delivered the value through val_ptr.
ccol_key_not_found     The key is absent and there is no getter, or
                       the getter failed.
ccol_invalid_args      cache is CLRU_CACHE_INVALID, or cache is a
                       stale handle that a call already destroyed, or
                       key is a NULL character pointer, which is not a
                       string.
ccol_not_enough_memory An internal allocation failed.
ccol_unexpected_failure
                       A remote getter fetched a value whose size does
                       not match the size of ValT. This applies to a
                       val type other than char*, where sizeof(ValT)
                       is the exact size the macro needs, and the
                       result is the same whether this call ran the
                       fetch itself or coalesced onto the fetch of
                       another concurrent caller of the same key. The
                       macro leaves val_ptr untouched.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro uses a GNU C statement expression ({...}), so it needs GCC
or Clang.
The cache never hands out a pointer into its internal storage; it
always copies the value out before it unlocks the segment's lock.
The value type may be const-qualified, such as const int or a const
struct type: the macro copies the value through a temporary of the
type without its qualifiers, so val_ptr can point to a plain variable
of that type.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
For a char* val type, the string that the macro puts in
*(char **)val_ptr is a heap copy that the caller then owns and MUST
free, with the cache's custom free function if a custom allocator was
configured and with free() if not, or the memory leaks. Each clru_get
call for a char* value makes its own allocation, so two calls for the
same key give two independent pointers, and the caller must free each
of them.

For a val type other than char*, nothing is allocated on the heap:
the value is converted into *val_ptr, and the caller must NOT call
free() on it.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
/* A value type that is not a string: val_ptr is a double *, not a
   cmap_pair *, and the macro copies the value straight into *val_ptr
   without any allocation on the heap */
clru_construct(cache, int, double, 128, NULL, NULL, NULL);

int k = 5;
double v = 2.71;
clru_set(cache, k, v);

double out = 0.0;
if (clru_get(cache, k, &out) == ccol_success) {
    printf("%.2f\en", out);
}

clru_destroy(cache);

/* A string value type. The caller must free the string it gets */
clru_construct(str_cache, int, char *, 64, NULL, NULL, NULL);

char *greeting = "hello";
clru_set(str_cache, 1, greeting);

char *result = NULL;
if (clru_get(str_cache, 1, &result) == ccol_success) {
    printf("%s\en", result);
    free(result);
}

clru_destroy(str_cache);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clru_set (3),
.BR clrucache_get_full (3)
